---
import FlowShot from '../FlowShot.astro';
import FlowDetail from '../FlowDetail.astro';
import FlowShot from '../../../components/flow/FlowShot.astro';
import FlowDetail from '../../../components/flow/FlowDetail.astro';
import shotBenchmarks from '../../../assets/flow/shots/flow-benchmarks-shot.webp';
import detailBenchRows from '../../../assets/flow/details/detail-benchmarks-rows.webp';
import detailBenchScoring from '../../../assets/flow/details/detail-benchmarks-scoring.webp';
import detailBenchPreset from '../../../assets/flow/details/detail-benchmarks-preset.webp';
import detailBenchMachine from '../../../assets/flow/details/detail-benchmarks-machine.webp';
import detailBenchMethod from '../../../assets/flow/details/detail-benchmarks-method.webp';
---
{/* Chapter 4b: Benchmarks. Written 2026-08-18 from the benchmarks brief and
    REVISED THE SAME DAY for 0134 A2, which re-shot the screen and changed
    what is true on it. Two A1 sentences this page carried are now false and
    were removed rather than softened: every installed model has since been
    measured, so the "a guess is labelled a guess" claim had no subject left
    on screen, and the screen now ranks three ways rather than one.

    Sits between the runtime chapter and the resource readout because that
    is the argument's own order: you own models (4), which of them should
    you actually run (4b), and what does running one cost your machine (5).

    A2 IS BUILT AND MACHINE-VERIFIED BUT NOT OPERATOR-ACCEPTED, and the shot
    is a development build. The scoring claims are shipped-in-repo, not
    released. If this page goes live before that acceptance lands, re-read
    the brief's build note first.

    VOCABULARY RULE, non-negotiable. The implementation says TTFT, prefill,
    decode, tok/s, KV cache and quantization. None of those may appear here.
    The published vocabulary is the screen's: time to first word, reading
    speed, how much it can read at once, fits this Mac. If a future edit
    needs a word, take it off the screenshot, not out of the repo.

    FIVE claims that must not be softened, each the reason the screen is
    trustworthy rather than decorative:
      * 300 GB/s is a PUBLISHED SPECIFICATION and is labelled that way. No
        public interface on a Mac reports memory bandwidth. Flow measured
        268.6 GB/s while running a model, and that is a different kind of
        fact. Never write that Flow read 300 GB/s from the Mac.
      * The 8K limit is FLOW'S, not the model's. Four of five models can
        read 262,144. It is a filed defect, reported not hidden, and must
        never be described as a model limitation.
      * There is NO central benchmark feed. Every number is measured on the
        reader's own Mac or computed from their own files.
      * A SCORE IS RELATIVE TO THE MODELS BEING RANKED, on this Mac. The
        best measured value on each axis becomes 1.00, so a score answers
        "which of these, here". It must never be published as a
        cross-machine or cross-catalog rating.
      * THE SCORE IS NOT A QUALITY VERDICT. It weighs speed, reading rate
        and headroom, which are the only axes Flow has measured. The screen
        itself prints "Not ranked: instruction-following", and this page
        must never imply the ranking judges how good a model's answers are.

    STAGE CORNER: --br. Pinned by both neighbours, not chosen freely: the
    previous stage is Chapter 3's --bl and the NEXT one is now Smart
    Routing's --bl (Chapter 4c, added 2026-08-18; before that the next
    stage was Chapter 7's --tr). --br stays distinct from both. An earlier
    --tr here collided with Chapter 7 and was caught by
    tests/e2e/product-stage.spec.ts, which asserts adjacency rather than
    global uniqueness — so re-check the ADJACENT stages, which are not the
    adjacent chapters, before changing this. */}
<section class="border-t border-border/60 px-6 py-14 sm:py-20" aria-labelledby="tour-benchmarks">
  <div>
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
      <p class="of-eyebrow" data-animate="rise">Benchmarks</p>
      <h3 id="tour-benchmarks" class="of-display scroll-mt-28 mt-4 text-[clamp(1.85rem,3.4vw,2.75rem)]" data-animate="lead">
        The fastest model on your Mac is probably not the one you picked.
      </h3>
      <p class="of-lede mt-5" data-animate="rise">
        Every local model comparison you can find was run on a machine you do not own. Flow
        measures the models <em>you</em> have, on the Mac you are sitting at, and ranks them by
        the thing you actually feel: how long you wait before the first word appears. Every row
        carries the date it was measured, and the screen states its own method, so you can
        check the ranking instead of trusting it.
      </p>
      <p class="of-lede mt-4" data-animate="rise">
        Bigger is not slower, and that is the surprise. On one M3 Max, a 20.4 GB model reached
        its first word in 4.5 seconds while an 8.5 GB model took 10.2. The larger one is
        2.3 times quicker to answer and reads three times faster, because of how it is built
        rather than how big it is. A list sorted by file size would rank those two exactly
        backwards.
      </p>
    </div>

    {/* The leaderboard, laid over the window it came from. TWO rows on
        purpose: the claim is a comparison, and one row alone is a number
        with nothing to weigh it against. */}
    <div class="of-stage of-stage--pad-bottom mt-12" data-animate="plate">
      <FlowShot
        src={shotBenchmarks}
        alt="Orionfold Flow's Benchmarks screen beside a client proposal: a preset row stating the active weighting, then five measured local models ranked by score, each with its time to first word, reading speed, fit verdict, and how much it can read."
        crop
        sizes="(min-width: 1280px) 78rem, (min-width: 1024px) 64rem, 100vw"
      />
      <FlowDetail
        class="of-stage__detail of-stage__detail--br"
        src={detailBenchRows}
        alt="The top two rows of Flow's leaderboard: advisor-gguf-q8_0 at 2.1 seconds to first word, 50 words a second, scoring 88 of 100, above qwen3.5-35b-a3b-4bit at 4.5 seconds and 84 words a second, scoring 64."
        caption="Two models, two different strengths · The quicker starter leads the faster reader"
        sizes="(min-width: 1280px) 30rem, (min-width: 1024px) 27rem, calc(100vw - 3rem)"
        animate
      />
    </div>

    {/* The machine strip. Short, wide, and centred under the stage because
        it is the caveat the ranking above depends on rather than a claim of
        its own: reading speed is a property of the chip, so the same model
        is quick on one Mac and unusable on another. */}
    <figure class="bench-machine mx-auto mt-14" data-animate="rise">
      <FlowDetail
        src={detailBenchMachine}
        alt="The machine strip beside Flow's Benchmarks title: Apple M3 Max, 36 GB memory, 30 GPU cores, 10+4 CPU cores, and 194 GB free of 926 GB."
        caption="Read from the machine · Anything the Mac does not report is left out"
        sizes="(min-width: 900px) 26rem, calc(100vw - 3rem)"
      />
    </figure>
    <p class="bench-machine__note mx-auto mt-5 text-center" data-animate="rise">
      The Mac sits beside the ranking, because a ranking of local models means nothing without
      the machine it was measured on. Reading speed depends on the chip, so the same model is
      quick on one Mac and unusable on another. A score compares these models to each other,
      here, and says nothing about any other machine.
    </p>

    {/* Three coupled claims about how the ranking is made and how it can be
        checked. Each pairs its copy ABOVE a full-width crop for the
        legibility reason recorded on .bench-honesty in the stylesheet
        below. Order is deliberate: what you can ask of it, how it answers,
        and what it refuses to answer. */}
    <div class="bench-honesty mx-auto mt-16">
      <div class="bench-honesty__item">
        <div class="bench-honesty__copy" data-animate="rise">
          <h4 class="bench-honesty__head">Rank it for the work you are doing.</h4>
          <p class="bench-honesty__body">
            Three settings sit above the list. Responsive puts most of the weight on how fast
            the first word arrives, Roomy puts it on how much memory is left over, and Balanced
            sits between them. Whichever you pick, the screen prints the weighting it is
            applying, right there beside the models. The reader never has to guess what
            "ranked" meant, and nothing is hidden in a settings pane somewhere else.
          </p>
        </div>
        <FlowDetail
          src={detailBenchPreset}
          alt="Flow's ranking control: three preset buttons with the middle one selected, then the active weighting stated inline as First word 50 percent, Reading 30 percent, Headroom 20 percent, each with its own coloured bar, above the line Ranked for balanced, measured on this Mac, and Not ranked, instruction-following."
          caption="The weighting is printed, not buried · And it names what it does not rank"
          sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 62rem, calc(100vw - 3rem)"
        />
      </div>
      <div class="bench-honesty__item">
        <div class="bench-honesty__copy" data-animate="rise">
          <h4 class="bench-honesty__head">Every score opens up and shows its arithmetic.</h4>
          <p class="bench-honesty__body">
            Open a row and the score breaks into the parts that made it: how each model stood on
            each measure, the weight applied, and what that contributed. The parts add up to the
            number on the row, because the number is nothing but their sum. Here the two leaders
            win different things. The first is quickest to a first word but a middling reader.
            The second is the best reader on this Mac and slower to start. One ranked list would
            bury that. The breakdown shows it, and shows exactly how the weighting settled it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <FlowDetail
          src={detailBenchScoring}
          alt="Two rows expanded in Flow's leaderboard. The first scores 88: time to first word 1.00 times 50 percent equals 0.50, reading speed 0.60 times 30 percent equals 0.18, memory headroom 1.00 times 20 percent equals 0.20, total 0.88. The second scores 64: 0.46 times 50 percent equals 0.23, 1.00 times 30 percent equals 0.30, 0.53 times 20 percent equals 0.11, total 0.64."
          caption="Standing, weight, contribution · The parts add up to the score"
          sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 62rem, calc(100vw - 3rem)"
        />
      </div>
      <div class="bench-honesty__item">
        <div class="bench-honesty__copy" data-animate="rise">
          <h4 class="bench-honesty__head">The screen states its own method.</h4>
          <p class="bench-honesty__body">
            Written from what actually happened in your session, never canned: how many models
            were run and when, and how much text was read. When two rows were measured on
            different amounts of text it says so plainly and tells you to measure them again
            before comparing, rather than quietly ranking figures that do not belong side by
            side. Where a number is a manufacturer's published specification the screen marks it
            as one, and Flow's own measurement sits beside it as a different kind of fact.
          </p>
        </div>
        <FlowDetail
          src={detailBenchMethod}
          alt="Flow's How this was measured section: 5 of 5 models were run on this Mac with the most recent date and time, and a How much was read entry reading that the rows were measured at different lengths, are not directly comparable, and should be measured again to compare."
          caption="Assembled from your session · It flags its own uneven comparisons"
          sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 62rem, calc(100vw - 3rem)"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <ul class="flow-list mx-auto mt-14 max-w-3xl">
      <li>A score ranks these models against each other, on this Mac. The best measured result on each measure sets the top mark, so the number answers "which of these, here" and nothing about anyone else's machine.</li>
      <li>The ranking is about speed and fit, never about how good the answers are. The screen says so out loud: instruction-following is named on screen as something it does not rank.</li>
      <li>A model that has never been run gets no score at all, rather than a zero that would rank it last as though it had been measured and lost.</li>
      <li>Nothing comes from anyone else's hardware. Every figure was measured on your own Mac or worked out from your own model files. Flow publishes no shared leaderboard and downloads no results.</li>
      <li>A model your Mac cannot open is still listed, with the reason. A model you can see in your library that vanished from this screen would read as a bug, so it never vanishes.</li>
      <li>Memory is a verdict, not a number: fits comfortably, fits, or too large for this Mac. The two engines Flow ships count memory so differently that a byte figure would invite a comparison the measurement cannot support.</li>
      <li>Every row names how much the model could read and how much Flow serves it here. That window is worked out per model, per machine: the smaller of what the model itself declares and what a set share of this Mac's memory can hold. Where a model could read more, the screen says so and names the limit as Flow's own, not the model's.</li>
      <li>This screen ranks what you already have. It does not recommend models, sell them, or fetch them.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="of-spec-fine mx-auto mt-8 max-w-3xl text-center uppercase tracking-[0.14em]">
      Measured 2026-08-18 on one Apple M3 Max, on a development build, through the runtime Flow ships. Your own Mac and your own models will produce different numbers, which is the point.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
